---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Questions, answered honestly — DuBose, M.D." active="questions">

  <!-- Intro -->
  <section class="hero" style="padding-block:clamp(48px,7vw,72px)">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-head">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
          <h1>Questions, answered honestly</h1>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">
          If your question isn&rsquo;t here — or you&rsquo;d rather just ask
          a person — the free conversation exists for exactly that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pillbar">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row">
        <a href="#model">The model</a>
        <a href="#money">Money &amp; coverage</a>
        <a href="#availability">Availability</a>
        <a href="#medicine">The medicine</a>
        <a href="#starting">Starting &amp; stopping</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="faq-group" id="model">
        <h2>The model</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Is this health insurance?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. It is a direct agreement between you and me for my services
              as your physician. It does not replace insurance, and I
              recommend every member keep coverage for everything outside our
              relationship — labs, medications, specialists, hospitals.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is this &ldquo;concierge medicine&rdquo;?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              It shares one idea with concierge practices — a small panel and
              real access — without the rest. Concierge fees typically run
              $2,400 to $5,000 a year on top of insurance billing. This is
              $100 a month, I bill no one, and there are no tiers. The formal
              name for the model is direct primary care.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Why is it only $100 a month? What&rsquo;s the catch?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              The price works because the practice is small by design: no
              office building, no billing department, no staff, and a limited
              number of patients. The limits are stated rather than hidden: I
              keep business hours, I am not an emergency service, I take real
              time away each year, and when my panel is full it is full. That
              is the whole catch.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I already have doctors I like. Does this still make sense?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Often, yes. You can keep every doctor you have — including
              another primary care physician. Members in that situation use
              me as the doctor who has time: to explain, to coordinate, to
              watch the whole picture between everyone else&rsquo;s
              appointments.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What does &ldquo;unhurried&rdquo; actually mean?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Visits are scheduled for as long as they need — a first visit
              is up to 90 minutes, and no visit ends because a schedule says
              so. I keep my panel small enough that this stays true.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="money">
        <h2>Money &amp; coverage</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Besides $100 a month, what will I pay?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Nothing to me. Care delivered by others is billed by them,
              usually through your insurance: labs, imaging, medications,
              vaccines, specialists, urgent care, hospitals. Where I can get
              discounted direct lab pricing, I pass it through at my cost and
              tell you the number before anything is drawn.
              More: <a href="/pricing">Pricing &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Are there extra fees for anything? Forms? Missed visits?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. Paperwork — disability and FMLA forms, letters, prior
              authorizations — is part of the job, not an upsell. There are
              no no-show fees; if scheduling stops working we will talk about
              it. The only pass-throughs are procedure supplies at my cost,
              named before the procedure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I&rsquo;m on Medicare. Can I join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes. Federal rules require one extra document — a Medicare
              private contract — because I have formally opted out of billing
              Medicare for my services. Your Medicare continues to pay for
              labs, imaging, specialists, prescriptions, and hospital care
              exactly as before. More: <a href="/pricing">Pricing &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I&rsquo;m on Medicaid. Can I join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes, with one signed acknowledgment required by state rules. If
              you have traditional fee-for-service Medicaid we will talk
              first about how tests and prescriptions I order are covered.
              One exception: federal rules bar private arrangements for
              certain combined Medicare-Medicaid programs (QMB) — if that is
              your situation, I will help you find good care instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I pay with my HSA?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Possibly — recent federal law may allow HSA funds for direct
              primary care fees like this one. Confirm with your tax advisor;
              I will give you the documentation you need.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if $100 a month is genuinely out of reach?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Tell me. I keep a limited number of reduced-rate memberships
              for people for whom the standard price would prevent care. It
              is a conversation, not an application.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="availability">
        <h2>Availability</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>How quickly do you respond to messages?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Within one to two business days, usually faster during business
              hours. If something needs attention sooner, say so in the
              message. If it is urgent or an emergency, see the next answer —
              messaging is never the path for those.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What about nights, weekends, and emergencies?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              I keep business hours, Monday through Friday, and I do not
              promise 24/7 access. Emergencies — chest pain, stroke signs,
              severe injury, thoughts of self-harm — are always 911 and the
              ER. Urgent same-day problems when I am unavailable: urgent
              care, then tell me and I will fold what happened into your
              care. This is what keeps the attention real for everyone the
              rest of the time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens when you are away?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              I take genuine time away each year. You will know in advance, I
              still check incoming results and messages periodically for
              anything important, refills are squared away before I go, and
              the emergency and urgent-care pathways work exactly as always.
              Away means slower — never unaware.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do you make house calls?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes, when an examination genuinely needs it and you are within
              my service area — scheduled, at no extra charge. Home visits
              are a clinical tool, not an on-demand service; most care
              happens by video, with clinic space when a procedure or exam
              needs a proper room.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="medicine">
        <h2>The medicine</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>What do you treat?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Full-scope adult primary care: blood pressure, diabetes,
              cholesterol, thyroid, everyday illness, prevention and
              screening, depression and anxiety, ADHD, weight management
              including modern medications, sleep problems, and the tangled
              situations where health and life affect each other.
              More: <a href="/help">What I can help with &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What is your training, exactly?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              M.D. from the Texas A&amp;M College of Medicine; internal
              medicine internship at George Washington University; years of
              part-time primary care practice alongside leading NIH-funded
              research on aging and access to care; currently pursuing
              lifestyle medicine certification. I did not complete a
              residency, and I will never imply otherwise — precision about
              my qualifications is where trust starts. The full picture,
              stated plainly: <a href="/about">About &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do you prescribe controlled medications?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Within a careful written policy, yes — including ADHD
              medications where appropriate. Federal and Texas telemedicine
              rules apply, some medications require in-person evaluation or
              monitoring, and I prescribe conservatively. If your primary
              goal is a specific controlled prescription, I am probably not
              the right fit — if your goal is to be well, we will use every
              tool that makes sense.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if I need a specialist or end up in the hospital?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Then you will have the right specialist, with a real referral
              and a doctor who stays involved — I coordinate, translate, and
              keep the whole picture. In the hospital, the hospital&rsquo;s
              physicians lead; I stay informed and manage your landing when
              you are home.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can you order labs and imaging?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes — ordered when needed, explained always. Labs run through
              your insurance or, where cheaper, at discounted direct prices I
              pass through at cost, told to you in advance.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="starting">
        <h2>Starting &amp; stopping</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>What is the free conversation like?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Thirty minutes by video. You tell me what is going on; I ask
              questions; I tell you honestly whether I can help. No forms
              beyond your name and email, no pressure, and no bill. If I am
              not the right fit, I will say so and point you toward someone
              good.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I join, and how fast can we start?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              After we talk: sign the membership agreement, add a payment
              method, and we schedule your first full visit — usually within
              the first two weeks, after I have read your records.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is there a contract or minimum commitment?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. Month to month from day one, no enrollment fee, cancel
              anytime effective at the end of your billing month.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens to my records if I leave?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              They are yours. A complete electronic copy is always free, sent
              promptly to you and your next physician, active issues are
              safely handed off, and prescriptions are bridged up to 90 days
              where clinically safe.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can my spouse or family join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Any adult can join — each with their own membership at the same
              $100. A couple is $200 a month. I care for adults only, so
              children need a pediatrician — I happen to be married to one,
              so I say that with respect.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing CTA -->
  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Still have a question? Ask me directly.</h2>
        <p>The conversation is free, and honest answers are the point.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-inverse" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
